00:00:02.320 --> 00:00:04.630 align:start position:0%
 
i'm honored and pleased to be the 2020  recipient of the napd  lifetime achievement award  it's special to be recognized by one's  peers  also i want to thank the members of the  selection committee for choosing me  and for the people who wrote  letters of recommendation  supporting my nomination  it's very much appreciated thank you  i don't have time to acknowledge and  individually all the people who have  contributed to the level of success that  i've had  but i do want to mention  my parents and other members of the  community where i grew up for their  support of education and me personally  also  warren goblin my major professor at  uw-madison who by the way turned 100  this past spring  and other mentors there  i was also fortunate to  be able to work with and to be the major  professor for  graduate students and post-docs that i  thoroughly enjoyed  you've done very good done very well  after  finishing your degrees and i'm very  proud of the accomplishments that you've  made  i chose to talk to talk on the topic of  perspectives of managing  change this should ring a bell with  those of us in  napb because indeed there's been a lot  of change just for the  meetings these last two years going from  in-person to virtual  i think the organizing committees have  done a really good job of doing that so  thank you very much  yes these times are changing  and  each time there's a change we have to  ask the question should i hang on to the  old or should i grab onto the new  change comes at us from all directions  when i first started working with  seminus vegetable seeds  i came home one evening and remarked to  mary that this job is a  director of worldwide breeding i think  it's going to be quite good and and fun  when things settle down a bit but guess  what they never really did settle down  there was always change going on i  should have expected this  but i didn't and so we've able to cope  with this  change comes from different directions  some is expected and some not  unexpected or unplanned  some is incremental and some is abrupt  some is widespread such as the global  climate change we're experiencing  sometimes we can initiate change  ourselves and that can be very positive  it's really this ongoing change that  creates much of the dynamic nature of  plant breeding but we have to be aware  of change in order to influence the  outcomes to our advantage  we also have to be able to cope with  change  be a person who brings  solutions not problems to the  organization keep an open mind put  yourself in other people's shoes don't  rush to judgment  practice continuing education use cross  training for yourself  for members of your team to keep you  updated to meet these challenges  when change comes and sometimes it does  and it's threatening it can be  overpowering  don't hesitate to help to seek help from  others and sometimes professionals  well each time we have these changes  that we encounter we have the  opportunity to either reject or delay or  to accept  either way we do it there's  opportunities for risk and reward life's  pretty much about risk and reward if we  miss opportunities sometimes that's a  big miss sometimes it's not so bad  because things turn out all right if we  accept we have to be ready to invest in  new time people and resources  should i hang on to the old or grab on  to do  that can be a difficult question in  every case  i've listed here  the personal changes that  i've encountered during my career as  plant breeding starting with the  education that i received  first from the university of nebraska  then  my graduate work at uw-madison and then  a post-doc at the university of  minnesota  my first job was at uw-madison where i  joined the faculty of the horticulture  department  working on vegetable crop research and  breeding  in teaching and  education after 22 years there i had the  opportunity to move to uc davis where i  joined the faculty of the pomology  department  working on fruit crops that was a big  change not only did we have to move from  the midwest to california but i was  working on perennial  crops rather than the seed propagated  annuals  it was quite different  i spent a decade there and then had the  opportunity to move to  the private sector with feminist  vegetable seeds  again working on vegetable crops but  quite different being in r d management  rather than research directly  after a dozen years there i decided to  retire and spent the rest of the time  since then as an independent consultant  each time i had decisions to make  individually along with members of my  family and others  each time we could either grab on to the  new challenge or not i did and i think  it turned out pretty well  there were other changes occurring  in plant breeding during my career  changes to the profession itself  when i began we were using primarily  phenotypic selection and quantitative  breeding  and focused a lot on progeny selection  and combining ability  and then the era of dna informed  breeding was ushered in when dna  sequencing and replication became  rather routine  and now we've integrated all of these  things we've added to the things that we  had with new ideas new technology new  science we're in the age of integrated  breeding  sometimes it was the science and  technology and the devices that drove  those changes other times there was  change that we had to innovate and  create new things in those areas  we're now in the era entering genome  editing and artificial intelligence  using big data sets and cloud computing  and i dare say there will probably be as  much change over the next 50 years as  there has been in the past  i want to spend a few uh some time here  with a few of the examples of change  that have occurred to me and  how i've addressed these changes  the first one that i recall was a big  biological change that occurred after i  just got back from nigeria in the early  part of my uh career at uw-madison  this was the  uh southern north southern corn leaf  blight epidemic of 1771.  it occurred very abruptly  and it was one of the most damaging and  costly outbreaks in agriculture in north  america  it was due to a confluence of an over  reliance on the texas or the t male  sterile cytoplasm  for the production of inver female  inbred lines for  seed production  and there was a mutant race of the  insight and pathogen that had been  rather dormant had been around that  hadn't been of much consequence for a  number of years  and it became very prevalent and caused  this outbreak  along with genetic uniformity  this raised the red flag about genetic  vulnerability from over the amount from  an overt amount of plant uniformity we  hadn't really talked about that much in  the past but it indeed became something  that we needed to consider  there were direct and associated changes  of this epidemic  to the seed corn industry where they had  to change from the use of the t  cytoplasmic male sterile parent seed  parents  began again de-tasseling the seed  parrots such as they'd done in the old  days  until they found new sources of  resistance which is now being used  there was some effects on other crops as  well because we looked at a lot of the  crops and found that they were in fact  genetically vulnerable there was too  much genetic uniformity  this led to the recommendations of  broadening the genetic base  but we found that there was in many  cases insufficient germplasm and  technology that we had at our disposable  at disposal in order to address these  issues  overall there was a good outcome of that  however because there was more there  were more resources that were allocated  for the collection  and the use of germplasm  and there were new programs created the  first time we really talked about  germplasm enhancement or pre-breeding  and these became rather common and  they're in use today  it also impacted my bean braiding  program  both the dry bean and the snap bean of  the garden bean cultivars had a very  narrow genetic base  they're having difficult time producing  new cultivars that were higher yielding  and better in the culinary aspects  more diverse germplasm was needed  in order to use that we'd be making wide  crosses and pedigree selection and  recurrent selection were of limited  value for this particular crop so a new  approach was needed  this led me to formulate the idea of the  inbred backross method which i published  in an article in hort science in 1981.  i'm shown here the stick diagram of the  process and the procedures  used in developing  inbred backross lines for replicated  trials and testing  well we know from experience that when  both parents are superior or equal and  relatively equal in genetic superiority  pedigree selection is preferred and it's  quite good been used for a long time  when one parent superior and one is  poorly adapted  one or more back crosses increases the  probability of recovering recombinants  that are better than the best parent so  we need that for selection in the inbred  back cross populations and indeed that's  better than pedigree selection in these  cases  i want to talk about a couple of other  uh  examples here  when i was the director of breeding at  seminas  the first occurred when our director of  st seed foundation seed stock and  foundation seeds became ill after a trip  abroad  he was severely ill and was out of work  for  six to nine months  but the department functioned pretty  well because they had undergone a lot of  cross-training and a lot of exposure to  each member of the  the department into the activities of  other members  so that went pretty well and they really  didn't miss a beat until he came back  in good health and could continue  the other situation in crisis management  occurred when  one of the associate directors of  worldwide breeding and a key breeder in  his group left rather unexpectedly  they turned in their letters of  recommendation on a monday morning just  as we were getting ready for a research  meeting  we weren't very pr well prepared for  that but we hadn't anticipated that and  we didn't know that was coming  and we didn't have anybody prepared to  step in on that it took a while to fill  these positions so  this led me to think in terms of how  could we make some changes and that led  to the concept of transition planning  what's transition planning do well it  considers the changes that we know will  occur but we don't know when or which  they'll be  it answers the question of who does what  when somebody's unable to do their work  because of illness or absence  and it also provides an opportunity for  each person to see what the other does  to find out what he or she does  put yourself in that person's shoes  if we do this in a good way and we can  standardize this there's some good  outcomes  there are many people or several people  in the department that understand can  handle each job of that team  it prepares the employees for other  responsibilities and opportunities  they may not have been aware of that  they had some talent for  and most of all it works pretty well for  both planned and unplanned changes  well we're in the midst of  at least three major changes that are  affecting plant breeding and i could  list many more if time allowed  we have the copa covet 19 global  pandemic that we're hopefully  on the way out of  in the midst of global climate change  which  we don't know for sure how long that's  going to last in the severity but we  know it's increasing  we're also from the standpoint of our  profession we're undergoing a change in  definition in the demographics  of the plant breeding profession in  other words who are the professional  plant breeders that are being trained  and moving into positions of  leadership  and our challenge is how to respond to  and manage amid these changes  well indeed the  two global changes are ongoing are  similar in some ways  but they're quite different in other  ways that i've listed here  the important thing to recognize is that  the plant breeding science and  technology and the phase changes that i  talked about  these weren't the drivers and aren't the  drivers of these change the change is  coming about from external factors  and we as plant breeders are being asked  to respond to this in the best way we  can to help mitigate the changes that  are occurring  we have to innovate and to adapt to  these  and hopefully i believe we can do that  with the  people and the tools and the technology  that we have  i think transition planning will help  especially in the case of  person power to meet these challenges  well i did i said that there were  changes in the profession itself and the  definition of demographics  plant breeding is indeed evolving from  what used to be solely cultivar breeding  it now includes many other activities  that i've mentioned here  such as germplasm enhancement and  pre-breeding we need to include all the  people that are contributing as breeders  a little bit different categories but  nevertheless they're essential and  should be recognized as breeders  plant breeding is changing from a white  male-dominated profession  beginning in the late 70s more women  were being admitted into graduate  programs and then moving into the  professional ranks of breeders  we're seriously underrepresented by the  minorities this is increasing slowly  and in each case we need more urgency to  meet the greater need for diversity  among the people in plant breeding  i would say that a goal is for all who  aspire to be breeders should be  encouraged and have equal opportunities  to achieve their goal i'll leave you  with a question i think you'll find  interesting when you see some of the  answers  that is  who was the first woman plant breeder in  your company your college or experiment  station when was she hired  i've done a little bit of work on this  and i found some things about people i  didn't really know about they made great  contributions and should be recognized  i'll just summarize in the following way  each of you are going to experience  change of all kinds it's going to come  at you in all directions  there's going to be direct and  collateral effects from these changes  sometimes there's a cascade of events  that give you some outcomes that you  hadn't expected  we need to learn both from the successes  and the crises that we face  to make new approaches for expected and  unexpected change  we need to embrace and support changes  to plant breeding profession and expand  the definition  of plant breeding and the people who are  being trained and educated moving into  positions of  of authority and direction  most of all enjoy your plant breeding  career as much as i have thank you very  much

